---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="VPN"
    categorySlug="vpn"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- What Is a Kill Switch? -->
  <h2 id="what-is-kill-switch">What Is a Kill Switch?</h2>
  <p>
    Picture this. You're <a href="/guides/do-i-need-vpn-cafes">in a cafe</a>, connected to VPN, sending client files.
    VPN hiccups for half a second.
    Without a kill switch? Your traffic instantly routes through the open network. Real IP exposed. Data
    readable. And you probably didn't even notice it happened.
  </p>
  <p>
    A kill switch cuts your internet the moment VPN drops. Nothing in, nothing out. Paranoid? Maybe. But
    I've seen what happens on cafe WiFi.
  </p>

  <!-- How Does It Work? -->
  <h2 id="how-it-works">How It Works</h2>
  <p>Pretty simple actually:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Kill switch watches your VPN connection constantly</li>
    <li>VPN drops (server restart, WiFi hiccup, whatever)</li>
    <li>All traffic blocked. Immediately.</li>
    <li>Stays blocked until VPN reconnects</li>
    <li>Back to normal</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    This happens in milliseconds. Even a half-second drop can't leak data because the kill switch is faster.
  </p>

  <!-- Why Remote Workers Need It -->
  <h2 id="why-you-need-it">Why You Actually Need This</h2>
  <p>VPNs drop more than you'd think:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Server updates (VPN companies do maintenance)</li>
    <li><a href="/tools/how-to-setup-vpn-all-devices">Switching networks</a> (WiFi to cellular, between access points)
    </li>
    <li>Crappy WiFi (hotels, airports, cafes)</li>
    <li>Random ISP issues</li>
    <li>Software conflicts on your machine</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What Leaks Without Protection</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Your real IP. Location revealed.</li>
    <li>Whatever you were sending. Readable.</li>
    <li>Login sessions. Yikes.</li>
    <li>Every site you're visiting</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On <a href="/blog/vpn-public-wifi-guide">public WiFi</a>, this happens in front of everyone. Including anyone
    specifically hunting on that
    network. It happens.
  </p>

  <!-- Types of Kill Switches -->
  <h2 id="types-of-kill-switches">Three Types of Kill Switches</h2>

  <h3>App-Level</h3>
  <p>
    Blocks only specific apps you choose. Browser keeps working, everything else too. Problem? Anything
    you forgot to add still leaks.
  </p>

  <h3>System-Level</h3>
  <p>
    Blocks everything. Complete protection. All apps lose internet when VPN drops. This is what I use.
  </p>

  <h3>Permanent</h3>
  <p>
    Blocks ALL non-VPN traffic, always. Even when VPN app isn't running. Can't accidentally browse
    unprotected. Very strict though—can lock you out.
  </p>

  <h3>My Take</h3>
  <p>
    System-level for most people. Complete protection without being annoying.
  </p>

  <!-- VPNs with Best Kill Switches -->
  <h2 id="best-vpns">Best VPNs for Kill Switch</h2>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={killSwitchColumns}
      rows={killSwitchRows}
      caption="VPN kill switch comparison"
  />

  <h3>NordVPN</h3>
  <p>
    Both app-level and system-level options. The system-wide one ("Internet Kill Switch") blocks
    everything. Tested it myself—works.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/nordvpn"
      text="Get NordVPN — Kill Switch That Works"
      variant="primary"
      program="nordvpn"
  />

  <h3>ExpressVPN</h3>
  <p>
    Calls theirs "Network Lock." System-level, extremely reliable. Caught every simulated drop in
    my testing. Zero leakage.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/expressvpn"
      text="Get ExpressVPN — Network Lock"
      variant="primary"
      program="expressvpn"
  />

  <h3>ProtonVPN</h3>
  <p>
    Has a permanent option. Your device literally can't access internet without VPN running. Great
    for <a href="/guides/secure-home-wifi-network">high-security situations</a>.
  </p>

  <!-- How to Test Your Kill Switch -->
  <h2 id="how-to-test">Test Yours. Seriously.</h2>
  <p>Don't assume it works. Test it:</p>

  <h3>Quick Test</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Connect VPN, verify your IP changed</li>
    <li>Open terminal, run continuous ping: <code>ping google.com -t</code></li>
    <li>Kill your VPN (simulate a drop)</li>
    <li>Pings should stop immediately</li>
    <li>Reconnect—pings resume</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>If It Fails</h3>
  <p>
    If your real IP showed up or pings continued? Kill switch isn't working. Check settings. Update the
    app. Or switch VPNs.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Recommendation -->
  <h2 id="recommendation">Bottom Line</h2>
  <p>
    Had a scare at a coworking space in Lisbon last year. VPN dropped for maybe ten seconds while I was logged
    into a client's admin panel. Didn't notice until later. Might have been fine. Might not have. Kill switch
    would've blocked everything instantly.
  </p>
  <p>
    So yeah. Turn it on. Settings, kill switch, enable. System-level if your VPN offers it. Takes about
    fifteen seconds.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your VPN doesn't have a reliable one? That's a reason to switch. <a href="/tools/best-vpn-digital-nomads">NordVPN
    and ExpressVPN</a> both get this right.
  </p>

  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/nordvpn"
      text="Get NordVPN — Kill Switch That Works"
      variant="primary"
      program="nordvpn"
  />

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
